-- v7 -> v8: the learned widths of the CURRENT and POWER dials (Part G §9.2).
--
-- Classic's CURRENT dial floored at +-60 A and POWER at +-10 000 W, VESC's own
-- RtDataSetup.qml defaults. A wheel cruises at ~6 A / ~571 W, so on an electric
-- unicycle both needles lived in the first tenth of scale and never visibly
-- moved. VESC Tool escapes this by rewriting both maxima from the motor config
-- (l_current_max, l_watt_max) once GET_MCCONF answers; Volty cannot read one
-- yet, and only one of the protocols it speaks has one at all. So the range is
-- LEARNED from what the machine reports, and these two columns are where it
-- lives between rides. See domain/stats/GaugeScale.kt.
--
-- REAL, because both are physical magnitudes the decoders already carry as
-- floats, and quantising them to the rung ladder here would throw away the
-- distinction the ladder itself needs (a peak that grows WITHIN a rung must be
-- retained, or it could never cross into the next one).
--
-- NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 is the whole three-state question answered, and answered
-- differently from dashboardStyle / yieldBmsToHeadUnit next door. Those two are
-- nullable because "unset" is a strictly different statement from any value they
-- can hold. Here it is not: a vehicle nobody has ridden and a vehicle that peaks
-- at 0 A both correctly open on the narrowest rung, so zero IS the answer rather
-- than the absence of one. DEFAULT 0 therefore reads history correctly too --
-- every vehicle that existed before this migration has, by definition, been
-- observed peaking at nothing, and each will learn its own scale over its next
-- ride.
--
-- Not per-protocol and not asked of the rider, both deliberately: the same
-- protocol drives a 250 A scooter and a 15 A wheel, and a current limit is a
-- number most riders do not know. The machine reports it by being ridden.
ALTER TABLE VehicleRow ADD COLUMN gaugePeakCurrentA REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
ALTER TABLE VehicleRow ADD COLUMN gaugePeakPowerW REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
